      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The hamlet’s name comes from a glass company whose products were hauled down the Glasco Turnpike to the Hudson for shipment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glasco%2C_New_York",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In its heyday, Glasco boasted more than six brick factories, a ferry, a school, churches, and several saloons.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glasco%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bricks from Glasco were barge‑loaded down the Hudson to build cities across America.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glasco%2C_New_York",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic Glasco Turnpike still winds past stone walls, farmland, and woodland, offering a scenic drive through the Catskills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
